.TH BOSWAS-CONTROL-CENTER 1 "November 2026" "boswas-control-center 1.0~alpha3" "Boswas OS"
.SH NAME
boswas-control-center \- settings hub of Boswas OS
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B boswas-control-center
.RB [ \-\-page
.IR page ]
.br
.B boswas-control-center
.B \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B boswas-control-center
is Boswas Control Center, the settings hub of Boswas OS. It has one page per
area. Each page shows the state of the device as the system reports it,
offers the actions that area supports, and opens the matching KDE settings
module with
.BR kcmshell6 (1)
for everything that is configured there.
.PP
The pages are Personalization (Boswas desktop presets and the KDE
appearance modules), Network, Bluetooth, Display, Sound, Power, Storage,
Applications (the Boswas Software Center), Windows Compatibility, Android,
Security (the Boswas Security Center), Users, Updates (the Boswas Update
Center), Devices, Privacy, Hardware & Drivers, System and About Boswas OS.
In a live session an Install Boswas OS page explains how to restart into the
installer.
.PP
The Security Center shows the local posture checks of
.B boswas \-\-json status
and the state of the Boswas device agent. Each item is shown as Secure,
Warning, Attention, Info, Not available or Error, always as text and as an
icon. The checks are a local self\-assessment of the device, not remote
attestation.
.PP
The Update Center shows whether automatic security updates are enabled and
when they last ran, and lists the pending updates (an APT simulation from the
package lists on the device, marking security updates). Check for Updates
refreshes the package lists and Install Updates upgrades the packages (never
removing any); both are administrator operations.
.PP
The Software Center lists the installed applications (Boswas, Windows and
the other desktop applications) and opens native applications with
.BR kstart (1).
Its other tabs search and install Debian packages from the signed
repositories, install Flatpak applications for the user from remotes the
administrator enabled, add AppImages (copied to ~/Applications; they run
without a sandbox), and route a chosen file by its content: Debian packages
to the package manager (with a warning that their origin is not verified),
Windows programs to the Compatibility Manager, Android apps to the Android
page.
.PP
The Android page shows the optional Android runtime (Waydroid) as reported by
.BR boswas\-android (1):
compatibility checks, installation, initialisation, start, stop, repair,
update and removal, the installed Android apps, and installing an APK file
after it was inspected. Google Play is shown as the runtime manager assesses
it; F\-Droid is offered as an alternative under its own name.
.PP
Hardware & Drivers lists the PCI and USB devices with the driver each one
uses (from sysfs; serial numbers are never read) and checks the kernel log for
missing firmware, offering to install the Debian package that provides it.
Storage shows the disks and file systems and frees space (package cache,
system journal, thumbnail cache). Network shows the connections and
addresses and switches Wi\-Fi and connections through NetworkManager; Wi\-Fi
passwords stay in KDE's network module. About Boswas OS includes a system
overview (load, memory, network, runtimes).
.SH SECURITY
Control Center runs as the desktop user and never as root. It starts only
the programs it knows (boswas, boswas\-preset, boswas\-android, kcmshell6,
kstart, kioclient, dolphin, partitionmanager, kinfocenter,
boswas\-compat\-manager, plasmashell, apt\-config, apt\-get (simulation only),
apt\-cache, flatpak, nmcli, lsblk, systemctl reboot in a live session, and
the polkit authorization tool), each with a validated argument list and a
timeout, and never through a shell.
.PP
Administrator operations go only to the Boswas system helper
.I /usr/libexec/boswas/sysops
(package boswas\-sysops), with an operation from a fixed list; polkit asks
for authorization. Nothing privileged runs when the window opens or a page
is shown: every change starts with a click and a confirmation that says what
will change. Success is reported only when the helper reports success.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-page " page"
Open the window at
.IR page :
one of personalization, network, bluetooth, display, sound, power, storage,
applications, windows, android, security, users, updates, devices, privacy,
hardware, system, about or install. The install page exists only in a live
session.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a short usage summary and exit.
.SH KEYBOARD
.TP
.B Up, Down
In the category list: switch pages.
.TP
.B F5
Refresh the current page.
.TP
.B Ctrl+F
Search the installed applications.
.TP
.B Ctrl+Q
Quit (an operation that cannot be interrupted safely finishes first).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /run/boswas\-agent/agent.sock
The Boswas device agent, used read\-only for the device, policy, privacy and
runtime status.
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/boswas/session.sock
The user's Boswas session service, used read\-only for the Windows
applications and the compatibility runtime.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/boswas/release
.TQ
.I /usr/lib/boswas/image\-info
The Boswas OS version and build shown on the About page.
.TP
.I ~/Applications
.TQ
.I ~/.local/share/applications/boswas\-appimage\-*.desktop
AppImages added in the Software Center and their menu entries.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 when the window was closed normally; 4 when started as root.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR boswas (1),
.BR boswas\-android (1),
.BR boswas\-compat\-manager (1),
.BR kcmshell6 (1),
.BR pkexec (1)
